.TH uniq 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
uniq \- report or filter repeated adjacent lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uniq
[\fB\-cdu\fR] [\fB+\fIn\fR] [\fB\-\fIn\fR] [\fIinput\fR [\fIoutput\fR]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uniq
copies
.I input
to
.IR output ,
dropping all but one of each run of
.I adjacent
equal lines.  Lines that are equal but not next to each other are not
noticed, so the input is usually sorted first.
.PP
With no file names it reads standard input and writes standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Prefix each line with the number of times it occurred.
.TP
.B \-d
Print only the lines that were repeated, once each.
.TP
.B \-u
Print only the lines that occurred exactly once.
.TP
.BI \- n
Ignore the first
.I n
fields, and the blanks before them, when comparing.
.TP
.BI + n
Ignore the first
.I n
characters when comparing.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# sort names | uniq
# sort names | uniq -c | sort -nr
# sort names | uniq -d       what is duplicated
.fi
.SH NOTES
Unlike GNU uniq, the second file name is an output file, not a second
input.  Naming a file that exists overwrites it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sort (1),
.BR comm (1)
